"A microscope magnifies tiny things.": "d8abda0d055d",
"A nanometer is one billionth of a meter.": "baf282d37ae9",
"At the very bottom are atoms. Silicon atoms in a chip sit about 0.24 nanometers from their neighbors. For sizes like this, scientists use an even smaller unit, the angstrom, which is one tenth of a nanometer. It is named after Anders Ångström, a Swedish scientist. So one silicon atom is a little more than 2 angstroms wide.": "16bffd399bc0",
"Compare the two pictures. What is different?": "b4f4eaf0dd6e",
"How big is a nanometer, and how big is an angstrom?": "3e9ad8c333e0",
"Keep going, and micrometers become too big. We need a smaller unit: the nanometer, one thousandth of a micrometer, or one billionth of a meter. A flu virus is about 100 nanometers across. The finest lines on a modern chip are a few tens of nanometers apart. A strand of DNA is only about 2 nanometers wide.": "7f479952a93d",
"Measure the desk with a ruler.": "5d23c7307edc",
"Names with angstroms work the same way. One chip maker uses names like 18A, and another uses A16, where the A stands for angstrom. They tell you which generation a chip belongs to, not what a ruler would read. Still, the real sizes are amazing. Chip makers now build parts only a few dozen atoms wide, billions of times on every chip.": "453b94feea0e",
"Now for a surprise. A “3-nanometer chip” does not have parts that are 3 nanometers wide. Long ago, the number in the name was a real length on the chip. Today it is only a name for a generation of chips: a smaller number means a newer design that packs in more transistors. In a “3-nanometer” chip, the closest metal lines are expected to be about 24 nanometers apart.": "83303a1f18c7",
"One nanometer is ten angstroms.": "044403495405",
"Start with your fingernail, about one centimeter wide. Zoom in about a hundred times and you reach a single hair, about 75 micrometers thick. A micrometer is one thousandth of a millimeter. Zoom in ten times more and you meet a red blood cell, about 7 micrometers across. A bacterium is smaller still, about 2 micrometers long.": "ea2938a3a179",
"The meter is a unit of length.": "a1c299157916",
"This phone is the newest generation.": "b51cae11e30d",
